##:
io/buf -- buffered reading and writing over any handle, pure Sushi.

The module ships as bundled .sushi source and is merged as a compilation unit when
imported (`use <io/buf>`). BufReader@(R) wraps anything that satisfies Reader and
BufWriter@(W) wraps anything that satisfies Writer, so a File and a TcpStream are
buffered by the same code.

A buffered read is one system call per WINDOW rather than one per line, which is the
whole point: File.readln() reads a byte at a time on a descriptor that cannot seek, and
reading a large file line by line that way costs one call per byte.

Neither type implements Reader or Writer, and that is a refusal rather than an omission.
A buffered read MOVES the cursor, so its receiver has to be `poke self`, and a perk
implementation must match its contract's receiver exactly (CE4004). Widening the
contracts to `poke self` is not available either: the console handles are File CONSTANTS
(ruling R15), and a `poke self` method on a constant is CE2400 -- so `stdout.write(...)`
would stop compiling. The two ways out, neither taken here, are a second perk for the
buffered direction, or receiver modes on a contract that a
constant can still satisfy.

Limitations: fill() is part of the surface because an extension method carries no
visibility marker -- it is as visible as the type it extends. buf_reader() and
buf_writer() answer IoError and never fail today; the channel is there so the call
composes with the handle it wraps. write() copies the string's bytes twice, once into
to_bytes() and once into the buffer.
:##

use <io/contracts>
use <io/fs>

##:
A reader that holds the last refill whole and hands lines and chunks out of it.

The window is the last read, held as bytes with a cursor into it. A read takes what the
window still has; only an exhausted window costs a system call. End of input is sticky:
once a refill has answered nothing, no further call reads the handle again.
:##
public struct BufReader@(R):
    ##: The handle being read. This value owns it. :##
    R inner
    ##: The last refill, held whole. :##
    u8[] window
    ##: How far into the window the cursor has moved. :##
    i32 pos
    ##: The refill size, in bytes. :##
    i32 cap
    ##: Whether a refill has already answered end of input. :##
    bool at_end

##:
Wraps a handle in a buffered reader, TAKING the handle.

The reader owns the handle from here: it closes when the reader is destroyed, and
into_inner() is how a caller gets it back.

- Parameter src: The handle to read. The reader takes ownership of it.
- Parameter cap: The refill size in bytes. Anything below 1 becomes 8192.
- Returns: The buffered reader.
- Errors: IoError, never today. The channel is here so the call composes with the
  handle it wraps.
:##
public fn buf_reader@(R: Reader)(nom R src, i32 cap) BufReader@(R) | IoError:
    let i32 size = cap
    if (size < 1):
        size := 8192
    let u8[] empty = from([])
    return Result.Ok(BufReader(src, empty, 0, size, false))

##:
Reads the next window from the handle, replacing the one held.

Every read here goes through this one call, so end of input is decided in one place and
stays decided. A caller rarely needs it; the reads below do.

- Returns: Whether any byte arrived. False means end of input.
- Errors: IoError, when the underlying read failed.
:##
extend BufReader@(R) fill(poke self) bool | IoError:
    if (self.at_end):
        return false
    let u8[] chunk = self.inner.read_bytes(self.cap)??
    let bool got = chunk.len() > 0
    self.window := chunk
    self.pos := 0
    if (not got):
        self.at_end := true
    return got

##:
Reads what the window still has, as bytes, refilling it if it is spent.

The answer is never longer than `max` and never crosses a refill, so a short answer is
not an end of input. An EMPTY answer is: that is what lets a caller loop until the
answer is empty.

- Parameter max: The most bytes to take.
- Returns: The bytes taken, empty at end of input.
- Errors: IoError, when a refill failed.
:##
extend BufReader@(R) read_bytes(poke self, i32 max) u8[] | IoError:
    if (max < 1):
        let u8[] none = from([])
        return none
    if (self.pos >= self.window.len()):
        if (not self.fill()??):
            let u8[] ended = from([])
            return ended
    let i32 avail = self.window.len() - self.pos
    let i32 take = max
    if (take > avail):
        take := avail
    let u8[] out = self.window.ss(self.pos, take)
    self.pos := self.pos + take
    return out

##:
Reads what the window still has, as text.

The bound counts BYTES, so a multi-byte character can be split across two calls. That is
today's answer and not the last one.

- Parameter max: The most bytes to take.
- Returns: The text taken, empty at end of input.
- Errors: IoError, when a refill failed.
:##
extend BufReader@(R) read(poke self, i32 max) string | IoError:
    let u8[] chunk = self.read_bytes(max)??
    return chunk.to_string()

##:
Reads one line, without its newline.

A blank line is Some("") and the end of input is None, so the two are never the same
answer. A last line with no newline after it is still a line, and the None arrives on
the call after it. The line is copied out of the window in bulk, never a byte at a time,
and a line longer than the window spans as many refills as it needs.

- Returns: The line, or None at end of input.
- Errors: IoError, when a refill failed.
:##
extend BufReader@(R) read_line(poke self) Maybe@(string) | IoError:
    let u8[] line = from([])
    let bool saw_any = false
    let bool done = false
    while (not done):
        if (self.pos >= self.window.len()):
            if (not self.fill()??):
                done := true
        if (not done):
            let i32 stop = self.window.len()
            let i32 at = -1
            let i32 i = self.pos
            while (i < stop):
                if (self.window[i] == 10):
                    at := i
                    break
                i := i + 1
            saw_any := true
            if (at < 0):
                line.extend_range(self.window, self.pos, stop - self.pos)
                self.pos := stop
            else:
                line.extend_range(self.window, self.pos, at - self.pos)
                self.pos := at + 1
                done := true
    if (not saw_any):
        let Maybe@(string) ended = Maybe.None()
        return ended
    let Maybe@(string) got = Maybe.Some(line.to_string())
    return got

##:
Reads everything left, as text.

The whole answer is held in memory at once, so a large input wants read_line() or
read() in a loop instead.

- Returns: Everything from the cursor to the end of input.
- Errors: IoError, when a refill failed.
:##
extend BufReader@(R) read_all(poke self) string | IoError:
    let u8[] all = from([])
    let bool done = false
    while (not done):
        let u8[] chunk = self.read_bytes(65536)??
        if (chunk.len() == 0):
            done := true
        else:
            all.extend(chunk)
    return all.to_string()

##:
Hands the handle back and ends the reader.

The window is destroyed here and the reader is spent: a later mention of it is refused
while compiling. Whatever was already buffered and not read is DISCARDED, so the handle
comes back positioned where the last refill left the kernel and not where the cursor
was.

- Returns: The handle, now the caller's.
:##
extend BufReader@(R) into_inner(nom self) R:
    return nom self.inner

##:
A line iterator over a buffered reader, which it OWNS.

next() answers Maybe@(Result@(string, IoError)): the outer Maybe says whether the input
has more, and the inner Result says whether reading it worked. The two are never the
same answer -- a blank line is Some(Ok("")) and the end is None.

The stop is STICKY. A read failure reaches the caller once, as Some(Err(e)), and every
call after it answers None. Nothing retries and nothing is skipped, so a loop over this
iterator cannot spin on a descriptor that keeps failing.
:##
public struct Lines@(R):
    ##: The reader being walked. This value owns it, and the handle under it. :##
    BufReader@(R) reader
    ##: Whether the end or a failure has already been answered once. :##
    bool stopped

##:
Turns the reader into a line iterator, TAKING the reader.

The iterator owns the reader from here, and the reader owns the handle, so one drop
closes the descriptor. A later mention of the reader is refused while compiling.

- Returns: The iterator.

- Example:
  ```sushi no_run
  use <io/fs>

  fn show(string path) ~ | IoError:
      let File f = open(path, FileMode.Read())??
      let BufReader@(File) r = buf_reader(nom f, 8192)??
      foreach(line?? in r.lines()):
          println(line)
      return Result.Ok(~)

  fn main() i32:
      match show("data.txt"):
          Result.Ok(_) -> return Result.Ok(0)
          Result.Err(_) -> return Result.Ok(1)
  ```
:##
extend BufReader@(R) lines(nom self) Lines@(R):
    return Lines(self, false)

##:
Answers the next line, or nothing when the input has ended.

- Returns: Some(Ok(line)) for a line, Some(Err(e)) for the ONE failure, None at the end.
:##
extend Lines@(R) next(poke self) Maybe@(Result@(string, IoError)):
    if (self.stopped):
        let Maybe@(Result@(string, IoError)) ended = Maybe.None()
        return ended
    match self.reader.read_line():
        Result.Ok(Maybe.Some(nom line)) ->
            let Maybe@(Result@(string, IoError)) got = Maybe.Some(Result.Ok(nom line))
            return got
        Result.Ok(Maybe.None) ->
            self.stopped := true
            let Maybe@(Result@(string, IoError)) done = Maybe.None()
            return done
        Result.Err(e) ->
            self.stopped := true
            let Maybe@(Result@(string, IoError)) failed = Maybe.Some(Result.Err(e))
            return failed

##:
A writer that collects bytes and hands them over a window at a time.

Bytes go into a buffer and reach the handle when the buffer has filled, when flush() is
called, or when the writer is destroyed. The buffered write is what makes writing a
line at a time cheap: one system call per window rather than one per line.
:##
public struct BufWriter@(W):
    ##: The handle being written. This value owns it. :##
    W sink
    ##: The bytes waiting to go. :##
    u8[] buf
    ##: The size at which the buffer drains itself, in bytes. :##
    i32 cap

##:
Wraps a handle in a buffered writer, TAKING the handle.

The writer owns the handle from here: it closes when the writer is destroyed, and
into_inner() is how a caller gets it back.

- Parameter dst: The handle to write. The writer takes ownership of it.
- Parameter cap: The size at which the buffer drains, in bytes. Anything below 1
  becomes 8192.
- Returns: The buffered writer.
- Errors: IoError, never today. The channel is here so the call composes with the
  handle it wraps.
:##
public fn buf_writer@(W: Writer)(nom W dst, i32 cap) BufWriter@(W) | IoError:
    let i32 size = cap
    if (size < 1):
        size := 8192
    let u8[] empty = from([])
    return Result.Ok(BufWriter(dst, empty, size))

##:
Sends everything waiting, then flushes the handle under it.

This is the repeatable checked drain: it may be called as often as a caller likes and
answers Ok when the buffer is already empty.

- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: IoError, when the write or the handle's own flush failed.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) flush(poke self) ~ | IoError:
    if (self.buf.len() == 0):
        return ~
    self.sink.write_bytes(self.buf)??
    self.buf.clear()
    self.sink.flush()??
    return ~

##:
Drains the buffer if it has reached its size. Every write goes through this one call.

- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: IoError, when the drain failed.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) drain_if_full(poke self) ~ | IoError:
    if (self.buf.len() >= self.cap):
        self.flush()??
    return ~

##:
Writes bytes into the buffer.

Nothing is promised to have reached the handle when this returns. flush() or finish()
is what promises that.

- Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: IoError, when a drain failed.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) write_bytes(poke self, u8[] data) ~ | IoError:
    self.buf.extend(data)
    self.drain_if_full()??
    return ~

##:
Writes text into the buffer.

- Parameter data: The text to write.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: IoError, when a drain failed.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) write(poke self, string data) ~ | IoError:
    self.buf.extend(data.to_bytes())
    self.drain_if_full()??
    return ~

##:
Writes text and a newline into the buffer.

One append rather than two writes: the newline goes into the same buffer, so a line
costs no system call of its own.

- Parameter data: The text to write.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: IoError, when a drain failed.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) write_line(poke self, string data) ~ | IoError:
    self.buf.extend(data.to_bytes())
    self.buf.push(10)
    self.drain_if_full()??
    return ~

##:
Ends the writer, sending everything waiting first.

This is the call that ends a BufWriter, and it CONSUMES: nothing can forget to flush
after it, because there is nothing left to forget with. The handle closes when the
writer is destroyed, which happens as this call returns.

flush() is the repeatable form. Use finish() where the writer is finished with, so that
the last drain's failure cannot be lost.

- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: IoError, when the final drain failed.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) finish(nom self) ~ | IoError:
    self.flush()??
    return ~

##:
Sends everything waiting, then hands the handle back and ends the writer.

The buffer is destroyed here and the writer is spent: a later mention of it is refused
while compiling.

- Returns: The handle, now the caller's.
- Errors: IoError, when the final drain failed.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) into_inner(nom self) W | IoError:
    self.flush()??
    return nom self.sink

##:
Sends whatever is waiting when the writer is destroyed, and drops the error.

A destructor has nowhere to put a Result, so a failure here is LOST. finish() is the
checked path and exists for that reason.
:##
extend BufWriter@(W) with Drop:
    ##: Drains the buffer, ignoring a failure. :##
    fn drop(poke self) ~:
        match self.flush():
            Result.Ok(_) -> return ~
            Result.Err(_) -> return ~
